  "narrative": "The 1970s was a transformative time for Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture, as they began to reject the Eurocentric beauty standards imposed upon them. The natural hair movement, which emerged in the 1960s but gained momentum in the 1970s, encouraged individuals to celebrate their kinky hair textures. Hairstyles like Afros, cornrows, and dreadlocks became symbols of black identity and pride. Protective hairstyles, such as Bantu knots and box braids, were also popular among Black women as a way to minimize hair manipulation and breakage. These styles not only showcased the beauty and diversity of African hair textures but also served as a form of resistance against the dominant culture's attempt to erase their identities. Unfortunately, documentation from this era is limited, and we can only speculate on the extent to which these hairstyles were adopted and celebrated by Black and African women during the 1970s. However, it is clear that the decade laid the groundwork for the continued growth of the natural hair movement in subsequent decades.",
